WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.400 --> 00:00:05.040
The Watcher takes audiences on another memorable&nbsp;
journey in Episode 2 of "What If...?" where&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:10.320
they’re treated to a story much different than&nbsp;
Peggy Carter's in Episode 1. Here’s Episode 2,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.440
explained.
 &nbsp;

00:00:11.440 --> 00:00:16.720
As the Watcher explained in the premiere episode,&nbsp;
one choice can create a new universe, which we saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.720 --> 00:00:21.360
when Peggy opted to stay on the floor to watch&nbsp;
the super-soldier experiment on Steve Rogers&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.360 --> 00:00:26.320
instead of going up into the booth. This time,&nbsp;
the focus was on T'Challa — voiced by the late,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.680
great Chadwick Boseman, who recorded his part&nbsp;
before his untimely death in August 2020.
 &nbsp;

00:00:31.680 --> 00:00:36.000
In Episode 2, the choice had a little more&nbsp;
humor behind it. We find out immediately when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:41.200
the episode begins that T'Challa is Star-Lord&nbsp;
— who in our reality is Peter Quill — after&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:45.440
he was wrongfully kidnapped from Earth by&nbsp;
the Ravagers. Instead of doing it himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:50.320
Yondu let Kraglin and Taserface find and take&nbsp;
the boy, which opened the door to this new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:54.880
universe. Being the complete morons that they&nbsp;
are, the men kidnapped the first boy they saw&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.880 --> 00:01:00.160
and figured it had to be him because he had "two&nbsp;
see holes, two hear holes, and one eat hole."&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.160 --> 00:01:04.800
Instead of returning T'Challa to Wakanda,&nbsp;
Yondu opted to keep the boy and raised him&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.800 --> 00:01:10.480
as a Ravager — who are Robin Hood-esque good guys.&nbsp;
Kidnapping T'Challa changed the entire universe in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:12.720
ways we could never have imagined.

00:01:12.720 --> 00:01:17.760
Episode 2 pulls characters from several different&nbsp;
films, including "Black Panther," "Guardians of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.960
the Galaxy," "Guardians of the Galaxy Vol. 2,"&nbsp;
and "Avengers: Infinity War." Fairly quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.960 --> 00:01:27.520
we learn that T'Challa convinced Thanos to&nbsp;
abandon his plan of murdering trillions by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.520 --> 00:01:32.240
teaching him how to reallocate the universe's&nbsp;
resources. Just like that, Thanos is a good&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:36.640
guy and aids the Ravagers in their mission to&nbsp;
steal the Embers of Genesis from the Collector.
 &nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:40.880
“Try calling me ‘Captain&nbsp;
Genocide’ again, new guy.”
 &nbsp;

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:46.160
Also aiding the group is Nebula, who rocks some&nbsp;
wavy blonde hair and appears to be in her original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:51.200
Luphomoid form — something we have never seen&nbsp;
before. She has some metal plating around her left&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:56.480
eye, but the rest of her body seems untouched.&nbsp;
Nebula and T'Challa have a flirty relationship,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.480 --> 00:02:00.240
which only reminds us that there is&nbsp;
no Nakia in this story — T'Challa's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:00.240 --> 00:02:04.400
initial love interest from "Black Panther."
 
However, one of the biggest takeaways here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:04.400 --> 00:02:09.040
that the Guardians of the Galaxy are never formed.&nbsp;
There's not even a new spin on the hero group, and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:09.040 --> 00:02:14.640
the episode is without Gamora, Groot, and Rocket.&nbsp;
We do get a brief scene with Drax, who bartends at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:19.440
an intergalactic club where T'Challa visits.&nbsp;
It turns out everyone knows who Star-Lord is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.320
in this story, and Drax thanks him for saving his&nbsp;
homeworld — which means his wife and daughter are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:24.320 --> 00:02:29.840
still alive. Drax is still a very blunt, literal&nbsp;
guy and tells T'Challa he looks terrible in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.040
photo together. Some things just never change.

00:02:33.040 --> 00:02:36.000
The big mission in the episode&nbsp;
is to steal the aforementioned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:41.200
Embers of Genesis — nutrient-rich cosmic&nbsp;
dust that can terraform entire ecosystems.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.200 --> 00:02:46.320
The good-natured T'Challa sees this as an&nbsp;
opportunity to eradicate hunger across the galaxy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.320 --> 00:02:50.560
but the rest of the Ravager team — which&nbsp;
includes Star-Lord superfan Korath — is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.560 --> 00:02:54.640
terrified to go up against the Collector&nbsp;
who keeps it in his library. Apparently,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.640 --> 00:02:59.600
the Collector is the new Thanos in this universe&nbsp;
after the Mad Titan "went straight." Yondu is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:04.880
terrified to go up against him, as are the rest&nbsp;
of the Ravagers as they dub the heist "suicide."
 &nbsp;

00:03:04.880 --> 00:03:09.680
With the Orb T'Challa stole from Morag earlier&nbsp;
in their possession, Nebula and Yondu are able&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:13.920
to get a meeting with the Collector — who is&nbsp;
totally jacked, by the way — so they can make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:18.240
their way into his vault on Knowhere. The Black&nbsp;
Order — who we initially know as the Children&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:23.440
of Thanos from "Infinity War" — now work for the&nbsp;
shirtless Collector in a bizarre twist. We finally&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.440 --> 00:03:28.000
meet the Collector — adorned with an eight-pack&nbsp;
that'd make Dwayne Johnson jealous — who appears&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:28.000 --> 00:03:31.840
much more ruthless than the white-haired&nbsp;
eccentric from "Guardians of the Galaxy."
 &nbsp;

00:03:32.560 --> 00:03:40.080
“Oh! That woman had taste! A Necrosword,&nbsp;
courtesy of the Asgardian Goddess of Death.”
 &nbsp;

00:03:41.280 --> 00:03:46.640
The team is successful in stealing the Embers, but&nbsp;
along the way, T'Challa discovers a Wakandan ship&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:46.640 --> 00:03:51.920
in the Collector's vault. He realizes his homeland&nbsp;
is still thriving and calls out Yondu for lying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:57.360
him about it being decimated. In the episode's&nbsp;
conclusion, the new gang travels to Wakanda,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.360 --> 00:04:02.320
where T'Challa is reunited with his family.&nbsp;
T'Chaka, Ramonda, and Shuri are there to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:02.320 --> 00:04:07.200
greet him. Okoye is also still around, with&nbsp;
her path unchanged as she still is a member&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:08.880
of the Dora Milaje.

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:13.360
T'Challa's role as Star-Lord effectively stops&nbsp;
the Snap from ever happening, and his Knowhere&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:13.360 --> 00:04:18.640
heist also allows for the entire universe to be&nbsp;
fed. That's a much bigger impact than Peggy Carter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.640 --> 00:04:23.440
becoming a super-soldier — no offense to our new&nbsp;
Captain. While that's the biggest takeaway from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.440 --> 00:04:28.560
Episode 2, there are plenty of "what if" scenarios&nbsp;
to wonder about that aren't laid out for us.
 &nbsp;

00:04:28.560 --> 00:04:31.760
There really isn't a need for the&nbsp;
Guardians of the Galaxy as we know them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:36.160
especially since Ronan wouldn't be looking&nbsp;
for the Orb to serve Thanos. The Kree leader&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:40.720
is probably off battling the Skrulls — who get a&nbsp;
brief mention in the club scene — but he doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:44.320
seem to be a threat in this universe.
 
Killmonger — who will appear in another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.320 --> 00:04:48.960
"What If...? episode — is still out there we&nbsp;
assume, but he hasn't arrived in Wakanda yet.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.960 --> 00:04:53.360
It's possible he still comes to challenge the&nbsp;
throne, because T'Challa becoming Star-Lord&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.360 --> 00:04:58.000
doesn't necessarily change his story at all.
 
Also, in the episode's conclusion, we see that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:03.040
Ego has traveled to Earth on his own and meets&nbsp;
Peter, who is mopping the floor of a Dairy Queen.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.040 --> 00:05:08.080
This story could go anywhere, with a naive Peter&nbsp;
much more likely to aid his planet of a father in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.080 --> 00:05:10.480
whatever he might be up to these days.

00:05:10.480 --> 00:05:15.040
One of the biggest things to ponder from Episode&nbsp;
2 are the deeper ramifications of T'Challa&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:20.320
convincing Thanos to abort his genocidal plan.&nbsp;
This would all boil down to timing and at what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.320 --> 00:05:25.040
point the Mad Titan decided to go straight. If&nbsp;
it was done before he began collecting stones,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:25.040 --> 00:05:29.600
or more importantly — before he obtained the&nbsp;
Mind Stone — it would change the entire MCU&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:34.240
from Phase 2 and beyond. Just go with us on&nbsp;
this one, as it might sound a little crazy.
 &nbsp;

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:38.560
Thanos gives Loki the Scepter — which holds&nbsp;
the Mind Stone — so he'd have the upper hand&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.560 --> 00:05:43.280
on Earth as he sought to obtain the Tesseract —&nbsp;
which contains the Space Stone. If Thanos gives&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:43.280 --> 00:05:48.320
up his plan before giving Loki the Scepter, that&nbsp;
means the Mind Stone never makes its way to Earth.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.320 --> 00:05:53.120
This simple change could suggest that the Avengers&nbsp;
never form because, without Loki, the team would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:53.120 --> 00:05:57.920
never come together. It's as simple as that.
 
If the Avengers were formed one way or another,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:57.920 --> 00:06:01.600
Tony Stark and Bruce Banner never would&nbsp;
have gotten their hands on the Mind Stone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.600 --> 00:06:06.560
which would mean no Ultron and no Vision. This&nbsp;
also means Baron Zemo is happily chowing down&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.560 --> 00:06:11.200
on some paprikash with his wife and children in&nbsp;
Sokovia. He never would have bombed the United&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:11.200 --> 00:06:16.320
Nations meeting in Vienna, meaning T'Chaka is&nbsp;
no longer in danger. No Mind Stone on Earth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:16.320 --> 00:06:21.920
also means no Scarlet Witch and Quicksilver. That&nbsp;
little shiny sucker shaped the MCU more than any&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:26.400
other stone, and if T'Challa was Star-Lord,&nbsp;
this all might have looked very different.
 &nbsp;

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:30.880
As we said, this would all depend on timing,&nbsp;
and if Thanos had already sent Loki to Earth,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.880 --> 00:06:34.640
all these points we've made are moot.&nbsp;
That's the point of this series, though,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.640 --> 00:06:39.600
isn't it? We're left to ponder the question,&nbsp;
“What if?” The Watcher would be so proud.

